## Short answer  
| Decorator | Proxy |
|-----------|-------|
| **Intent** – *Add responsibilities to an object at runtime* | **Intent** – *Control access to, manage creation of, or represent another object* |
| **Typical use‑case** – Logging, caching, validation, theming, aspect‑like concerns | **Typical use‑case** – Virtual (lazy) initialization, remote object representation, protection, smart‑pointer‑style tracking |
| **What it changes** – The *behavior* of the target (method calls get wrapped, new logic runs before/after) | What it *does* – It may forward, block, or modify calls, or even decide not to instantiate the target at all |
| **Target** – Often a concrete instance that already exists | The target may be *created* by the proxy (lazy), or already exist; the proxy may be *remote* or *local* |
| **Relationship to target** – Composes the target; both share the same interface | Composes the target; usually the same interface but the proxy can stand in for it, hide its existence, or pretend to be it |

---

## 1. The Decorator Pattern

| Aspect | Details |
|--------|---------|
| **Definition** | A structural pattern that wraps an object to add new behavior while keeping the original interface intact. |
| **Intent** | *Attach additional responsibilities to an object dynamically* without subclassing. |
| **Structure** | ```text
Interface Component { … }
ConcreteComponent implements Component { … }
Decorator implements Component {
    Component component;
    … // overrides methods, delegates to component, adds new logic
}
ConcreteDecoratorX implements Component { … }
```
| **Typical Responsibilities Added** | Logging, timing, security checks, caching, validation, UI embellishment, protocol conversion, etc. |
| **When to use** | When you want to **extend** behavior of individual objects **without** affecting other objects, and you want to do it at **runtime**. |
| **Advantages** | - Open/closed principle (no changes to existing classes)<br>- Combines behavior by stacking decorators<br>- Fine‑grained control over which objects get which features |
| **Disadvantages** | - Can lead to many small classes<br>- Can obscure the core object’s responsibilities if overused |
| **Examples** | Java’s `java.io.FilterInputStream`/`FilterOutputStream`; `javax.swing.JComponent` and its `Border`/`ScrollPane` decorators; `java.util.logging.Logger` with `Handler`s. |

### Quick code snippet (Java)

```java
interface Text {
    String getContent();
}

class PlainText implements Text {
    public String getContent() { return "Hello"; }
}

class TextDecorator implements Text {
    protected Text wrapped;
    public TextDecorator(Text wrapped) { this.wrapped = wrapped; }
    public String getContent() { return wrapped.getContent(); }
}

class UpperCaseDecorator extends TextDecorator {
    public UpperCaseDecorator(Text wrapped) { super(wrapped); }
    public String getContent() { return super.getContent().toUpperCase(); }
}

// Usage
Text t = new UpperCaseDecorator(new PlainText());
System.out.println(t.getContent()); // "HELLO"
```

